[{:seon.task/name "an-identity-costs-its-seek"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes). A :db/unique identity datom costs 8.3k ticks and 19 KB and a content-id one 12.9k ticks and 43 KB (measured 2026-11-10 at 100 datoms) against a plain datom's 7.2k and 11.1 KB (docs/storage.md), because datahike.db.transaction resolves one [a v] by four seeks (content-tempids, the tempid branch, the content-id search, validate-datom-upsert) plus a sort-by and a hasch uuid an entity. Fix in the Datahike fork: resolve an identity's [a v] once a transaction and keep it in the report. Proof: seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek with its :overrun deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/an-identity-costs-its-seek}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-supplied-instant-is-not-earlier-than-the-last"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Deep fixes (an open finding of the merge-safety inventory, 2026-11-11). What exists: Datahike stamps a transaction's ``:db/txInstant`` with ``datahike.db.transaction/next-tx-instant``, the later of the wall clock and one millisecond past the instant of db-before's last transaction (its :max-tx's; ``reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/db/transaction.cljc`` near lines 235-266), so it strictly increases along one db line; but ``:db/txInstant`` supplied in a transaction's tx-meta wins over it unchecked (the merge near line 1373), so one write can back-date itself and the order by time that the owner ruled (docs/decisions.md, 2026-11-11: order by [:db/txInstant, tx id]; docs/storage.md#history) breaks on that line silently. Seon supplies none today; a merge's carried transactions keep their own instants by carrying their datoms, not through tx-meta. Correct, in the fork: a supplied instant earlier than or equal to the last is refused with a typed error naming both, unless the write says it back-dates (an explicit tx-meta key for imports, Datahike's own historical-import case), so a back-dated transaction is visible as one. Proof to write: a Datahike test in the fork (``datahike.test.transact-test``), red first on d32173c3: an earlier supplied instant refused, the same with the back-dating key accepted, an auto-stamped one unchanged; and its unit in test/forks/baseline.edn."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-keeps-mains-instants-rising"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). ``datahike.db.transaction/next-tx-instant`` gives max(clock, the instant of db-before's max-tx + 1 ms), and a :db/txInstant supplied in tx-meta wins with no check; a merge carries the branch's transaction entities with their instants, so main's instants no longer rise with its t and the merge's own instant can precede a carried one (measured with the clock pinned: three branch transactions and one on main, merged). The decision (2026-11-11): an import keeps load-time instants, and a supplied :db/txInstant is refused with a typed error unless later than the last: no back-dating path. Correct: a carried transaction's instant on the target is the merge's (load time), its branch instant kept as data of its own if a reader needs it, and a supplied :db/txInstant at or before the last is :transact/back-dated. Owner: Deep fixes (the fork)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/mains-instants-rise-with-its-t-after-a-merge-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-value-compares-by-content"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). ``datahike.db.transaction`` transact-report decides a card-one write changed its value with not=, which on Jolt compares byte arrays by identity: writing the bytes an entity holds retracts and asserts them again (measured on a vector's :seon.vector/f16), while the index compares arrays by content (``datahike.datom`` compare-arrays). Correct: the same-value check uses the index's comparison, so an equal value is a no-op for every type. Owner: Deep fixes (the fork)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/byte-values-compare-by-content-broken}}
]
